as 4 left. So W7 has slightly better chance to draw a third. But I already have two pairs, so I don't necessarily need another pair/pung; I need a meld. The quickest meld is from W2-W3 chow. After that, I could try to get a third B3 or B6 for pung meld. So maybe keep both pairs and hope to draw a third for one of them. Then my hand would be: meld from W2-W3 chow (if draw W1/W4), meld from B3 pung (if draw B3), pair from B6, and I already have two melds (J1 pung, B2-B4 chow). That would complete hand. So I need one chow and one pung from hand. So discard the tile that least helps.\n\nThus, discard T3 or W7. Which is more dangerous to discard? Game history: next player played T3 earlier, so they might not need T3. Previous player played T2 and T7. Not clear.\n\nMaybe discard T3 because it's a simple tile, less likely to be needed by others for big patterns.\n\nAlso, consider that B3 and B6 are safe? B3 was used in my chow meld, but that was from my own chi? Actually history shows \"Chi B3\" meaning I claimed a B3 to complete chow B2-B3-B4. So B3 is recently used, maybe safe.\n\nI'll choose to discard T3.\n\nReason: Keeping W2-W3 offers the best chance to complete a chow with one draw, and keeping both B3 and B6 pairs provides flexibility for forming a pung or using as final pair. T3 is isolated and less efficient to develop into a meld compared to other options.\n\nAnswer: Play T3."}
