B6 now), then on win we could have four B6 split into two pairs \u2192 \u201cTile Hog\u201d (2 points). But that\u2019s not enough alone.  \n- Two Dragon Pungs? Only J1 is dragon pung, J2 is not pung yet.  \n- No Honors? No \u2014 J1 pung exists.  \n- Mixed Double Chow? Not relevant.  \n- Pung of Terminals or Honors = yes (J1 pung already) = 2.  \n\nOur current two candidates:  \n1. Play B6: keep J2 pair; then hand is [B6, B6, J2, J2], waiting for B6 to make pung or four for tile hog, or J2 to make pung (but then need another pair). If we draw J2, we get J2 pung, need one more pair (like B6 pair). If we draw B6, we get B6 pung and J2 pair \u2192 winning hand (4 melds: J1 pung, B2-B3-B4 chow, W2-W3-W4 chow, B6 pung; pair: J2) \u2192 win.  \n\n2. Play J2: keep B6, B6, B6, J2, waiting for J2 (to make pair) or B6 (to make kong) or another tile to complete meld? Actually if we play J2, hand is [B6,B6,B6,J2], still need one meld+pair. If we draw J2 \u2192 pair J2 + B6 pung \u2192 same win as above. If we draw B6 \u2192 B6 kong (4th B6) \u2192 we then need a pair (only J2 left, no pair). Not good; would need a pair tile later, which may be hard given tile counts.  \n\nSo discarding J2 leaves us with 3 B6 + 1 J2, needing another J2 or something else for pair. Discarding B6 leaves us with 2 B6 + 2 J2, which is one pair (J2) + 1 B6 pair potential if draw B6, or J2 pung if draw J2.  \n\nLet\u2019s see tile counts:  \n- B6: 3 left in wall. Good chance to draw.  \n- J2: 3 left in wall. Good chance.  \n\nBut if we keep both pairs (B6 pair + J2 pair), that\u2019s 4 tiles; we still need 1 meld. But our three melds already exist, so with one more meld (say B6 pung) we win. If we have B6 pung + J2 pair: we win. If we have J2 pung + B6 pair: we win.  \nThus, discarding J2 means we give up J2 pair possibility immediately, forcing us to find a pair from elsewhere (less likely). Discarding B6 preserves both pair possibilities (J2 pair + B6 as potential pung).  \n\nAlso if we discard J2 now, another player might peng J2 (since J2 count left 3, but possible), giving them points. Discarding B6 less risky \u2014 B6 not likely to be called by others as pung since many B6 are still in wall.  \n\nFrom scoring:  \nWin with: J1 pung (2) + possibly another pung of J2 (another Dragon Pung = 2 more, total 4) + maybe concealed? No, all exposed. We\u2019d need more patterns.  \nBut we can also count:  \n- Two Dragon Pungs = 6 points (J1 and J2 pungs)  \n- That plus Dragon Pung (2) maybe overlapping? Actually Two Dragon Pungs includes both. So that\u2019s 6.  \n- Pung of Terminals or Honors = 2 for J1, 2 for J2? That\u2019s 4 total, but Two Dragon Pungs supersedes single dragon pung counts? Possibly can\u2019t double-count same meld for both patterns; but rules allow Dragon Pung (2) per meld and Two Dragon Pungs (6) if two dragon pungs. So 6 points from Two Dragon Pungs already, plus maybe Tile Hog if we keep four B6 as two pairs (but that\u2019s not our case here because we\u2019d form B6 pung).  \nActually our target win scenario:  \nHand: melds: J1 pung, B2-B3-B4 chow, W2-W3-W4 chow, B6 pung; pair: J2.  \nScoring:  \n- Two Dragon Pungs? No, only J1 pung is dragon; J2 pair is not pung. So only 1 dragon pung (2 points).  \n- Pung of Terminals or Honors (2 for J1 pung) = 2 points.  \n- Chicken Hand? Possible if no other scoring except Dragon Pung (2), less than 8. So not a valid win.  \nSo we need J2 pung, not pair, to get Two Dragon Pungs (6) + possibly others.  \n\nSo we must aim for: 4 melds: J1 pung, B2-B3-B4 chow, W2-W3-W4 chow, J2 pung; pair: B6 pair. That\u2019s 6 points (Two Dragon Pungs) + maybe others? Possibly All Chows? No, J1 pung not chow. Mixed Triple Chow? Not relevant. So total 6, still less than 8.  \n\nBut maybe with Self-Drawn (1), Edge/Closed Wait (1), etc., can reach 8.  \nHowever, given state, likely fastest win is to keep both J2 and B6 for flexibility, discard one to maintain tenpai.  \n\nActually, we\u2019re not in tenpai yet because we have 5 tiles, need 2 more to complete 14 tiles total (3 melds = 9 tiles + our 5 = 14). Wait \u2014 total tiles in hand + melds = 9 meld tiles + 5 hand tiles = 14, yes we are in tenpai if our 5 tiles form one meld + one pair. Currently: B6 B6 B6 J2 J2 \u2014 that\u2019s one pung (B6) + one pair (J2) = meld + pair \u2192 yes, we are in tenpai!  \nWe are waiting for:  \nIf we keep as is, we win if we draw: J2 (for J2 pung, B6 pair), or B6 (for B6 kong, J2 pair) \u2014 but B6 kong would use 4 B6, leaving only 1 J2, no pair, so not winning. So actually waiting only for J2.  \n\nSo tenpai: hand = [B6, B6, B6, J2, J2] waiting for J2. Discarding anything breaks tenpai. But actions given are Play J2 or Play B6. That means we must discard one tile. So we break tenpai.  \n\nNow: if discard J2, new hand = [B6,B6,B6,J2], need J2 or B6 plus another tile to form meld+pair. Not tenpai.  \nIf discard B6, new hand = [B6,B6,J2,J2], tenpai waiting for B6 (to form B6 pung, J2 pair) or J2 (to form J2 pung, B6 pair). Yes, that\u2019s still tenpai (two-sided wait).  \n\nSo discard B6 keeps tenpai with two waiting tiles; discard J2 breaks tenpai completely.  \n\nThus clearly **Play B6** is better to keep tenpai.  \n\nGiven prevailing wind South (F2), seat wind West (F3): we have no pungs of those. No extra points from winds.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nDiscarding B6 keeps tenpai (waiting for J2 or B6), while discarding J2 loses tenpai. Also, discarding J2 risks another player forming J2 pung (though 3 J2 left). We want to maximize chance to win with minimal risk. B6 is safer discard and maintains ready hand.  \n\n**Answer:** Play B6"}
